<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A6.T18.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 18</span>: </span><span id="A6.T18.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> Comparing compute requirements during training and inference throughput with state-of-the-art generative models. Compute during training in V100-days, numbers of competing methods taken from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2105-05233</a></cite> unless stated differently;<sup id="A6.T18.9.1" class="ltx_sup">∗</sup>: Throughput measured in samples/sec on a single NVIDIA A100;<sup id="A6.T18.9.2" class="ltx_sup">†</sup>: Numbers taken from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2105-05233</a></cite> ;<sup id="A6.T18.9.3" class="ltx_sup">‡</sup>: Assumed to be trained on 25M train examples; <sup id="A6.T18.9.4" class="ltx_sup"><span id="A6.T18.9.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">††</span></sup>: R-FID vs. ImageNet validation set</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Tab <a href="#A6.T18" title="Table 18 ‣ Appendix F Computational Requirements ‣ High-Resolution Image Synthesis with Latent Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a> we provide a more detailed analysis on our used compute ressources and compare our best performing models on the CelebA-HQ, FFHQ, LSUN and ImageNet datasets with the recent state of the art models by using their provided numbers, <em id="A6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cf</em>.<span id="A6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2105-05233</a></cite>. As they report their used compute in V100 days and we train all our models on a single NVIDIA A100 GPU, we convert the A100 days to V100 days by assuming a <math id="A6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times 2.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times 2.2</annotation></semantics></math> speedup of A100 vs V100 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">a100tov100</a></cite><span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          This factor corresponds to the speedup of the A100 over the V100 for a U-Net, as defined in Fig. 1 in  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">a100tov100</a></cite></span></span></span>. To assess sample quality, we additionally report FID scores on the reported datasets. We closely reach the performance of state of the art methods as StyleGAN2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">DBLP:journals/corr/abs-1912-04958</a></cite> and ADM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2105-05233</a></cite> while significantly reducing the required compute resources.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="A7" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix G </span>Details on Autoencoder Models</h2>

<div id="A7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We train all our autoencoder models in an adversarial manner following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2012-09841</a></cite>, such that a patch-based discriminator <math id="A7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math> is optimized to differentiate original images from reconstructions <math id="A7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}(\mathcal{E}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}(\mathcal{E}(x))</annotation></semantics></math>.
To avoid arbitrarily scaled latent spaces, we regularize the latent <math id="A7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> to be zero centered and obtain small variance by introducing an regularizing loss term <math id="A7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{reg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{reg}</annotation></semantics></math>. 
<br class="ltx_break">We investigate two different regularization methods: (i) a low-weighted Kullback-Leibler-term between <math id="A7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\mathcal{E}}(z|x)=\mathcal{N}(z;\mathcal{E}_{\mu},\mathcal{E}_{\sigma^{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\mathcal{E}}(z|x)=\mathcal{N}(z;\mathcal{E}_{\mu},\mathcal{E}_{\sigma^{2}})</annotation></semantics></math> and a standard normal distribution
<math id="A7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(z;0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(z;0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> as in a standard variational autoencoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">VAE</a>; <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">VAE2</a></cite>, and, (ii) regularizing the latent space with a vector quantization layer by learning a codebook of <math id="A7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{Z}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{Z}|</annotation></semantics></math> different exemplars <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">DBLP:conf/nips/OordVK17</a></cite>. 
<br class="ltx_break">To obtain high-fidelity reconstructions we only use a very small regularization for both scenarios, <em id="A7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="A7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text"></span> we either weight the <math id="A7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕂</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝕃</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{KL}</annotation></semantics></math> term
by a factor <math id="A7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim 10^{-6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 10^{-6}</annotation></semantics></math> or choose a high codebook dimensionality <math id="A7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{Z}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{Z}|</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The full objective to train the autoencoding model <math id="A7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{E},\mathcal{D})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{E},\mathcal{D})</annotation></semantics></math> reads:</p>
<table id="A7.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A7.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{Autoencoder}}=\min_{\mathcal{E},\mathcal{D}}\max_{\psi}\Big(L_{rec}(x,\mathcal{D}(\mathcal{E}(x)))-L_{adv}(\mathcal{D}(\mathcal{E}(x)))+\log D_{\psi}(x)+L_{reg}(x;\mathcal{E},\mathcal{D})\Big)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>Autoencoder</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>ψ</mi></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{Autoencoder}}=\min_{\mathcal{E},\mathcal{D}}\max_{\psi}\Big(L_{rec}(x,\mathcal{D}(\mathcal{E}(x)))-L_{adv}(\mathcal{D}(\mathcal{E}(x)))+\log D_{\psi}(x)+L_{reg}(x;\mathcal{E},\mathcal{D})\Big)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">DM Training in Latent Space</h5>

<div id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Note that for training diffusion models on the learned latent space, we again distinguish two cases
when learning <math id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(z)</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(z|y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(z|y)</annotation></semantics></math> (Sec. <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Conditional Latent Diffusion ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ High-Resolution Image Synthesis with Latent Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>):
(i) For a KL-regularized latent space, we sample <math id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=\mathcal{E}_{\mu}(x)+\mathcal{E}_{\sigma}(x)\cdot\varepsilon=:\mathcal{E}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>μ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=\mathcal{E}_{\mu}(x)+\mathcal{E}_{\sigma}(x)\cdot\varepsilon=:\mathcal{E}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
When rescaling the latent, we estimate the component-wise variance</p>
<table id="A7.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A7.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}^{2}=\frac{1}{bchw}\sum_{b,c,h,w}(z^{b,c,h,w}-\hat{\mu})^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}^{2}=\frac{1}{bchw}\sum_{b,c,h,w}(z^{b,c,h,w}-\hat{\mu})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">from the first batch in the data, where <math id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mu}=\frac{1}{bchw}\sum_{b,c,h,w}z^{b,c,h,w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>μ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mu}=\frac{1}{bchw}\sum_{b,c,h,w}z^{b,c,h,w}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The output of <math id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is scaled such that the rescaled latent has unit standard deviation, <em id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text"></span> <math id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\leftarrow\frac{z}{\hat{\sigma}}=\frac{\mathcal{E}(x)}{\hat{\sigma}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mfrac><mi>z</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\leftarrow\frac{z}{\hat{\sigma}}=\frac{\mathcal{E}(x)}{\hat{\sigma}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
(ii) For a VQ-regularized latent space, we extract <math id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">before</em> the quantization layer and absorb the quantization operation
into the decoder, <em id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e</em>.<span id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.5" class="ltx_text"></span> it can be interpreted as the first layer of <math id="A7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A8" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix H </span>Additional Qualitative Results</h2>

<div id="A8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we provide additional qualitative results for our landscapes model (Fig. <a href="#A0.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ High-Resolution Image Synthesis with Latent Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>, <a href="#A8.F23" title="Figure 23 ‣ Appendix H Additional Qualitative Results ‣ High-Resolution Image Synthesis with Latent Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>, <a href="#A8.F24" title="Figure 24 ‣ Appendix H Additional Qualitative Results ‣ High-Resolution Image Synthesis with Latent Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a> and <a href="#A8.F25" title="Figure 25 ‣ Appendix H Additional Qualitative Results ‣ High-Resolution Image Synthesis with Latent Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>), our class-conditional ImageNet model (Fig. <a href="#A8.F26" title="Figure 26 ‣ Appendix H Additional Qualitative Results ‣ High-Resolution Image Synthesis with Latent Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a> - <a href="#A8.F27" title="Figure 27 ‣ Appendix H Additional Qualitative Results ‣ High-Resolution Image Synthesis with Latent Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>) and our unconditional models for the CelebA-HQ, FFHQ and LSUN datasets (Fig. <a href="#A8.F28" title="Figure 28 ‣ Appendix H Additional Qualitative Results ‣ High-Resolution Image Synthesis with Latent Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a> - <a href="#A8.F31" title="Figure 31 ‣ Appendix H Additional Qualitative Results ‣ High-Resolution Image Synthesis with Latent Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a>).
Similar as for the inpainting model in Sec. <a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5 Inpainting with Latent Diffusion ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ High-Resolution Image Synthesis with Latent Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> we also fine-tuned the semantic landscapes model from Sec. <a href="#S4.SS3.SSS2" title="4.3.2 Convolutional Sampling Beyond 
            
              
                
                256
                2 ‣ 4.3 Conditional Latent Diffusion ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ High-Resolution Image Synthesis with Latent Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3.2</span></a> directly on <math id="A8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>512</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> images and depict qualitative
results in Fig. <a href="#A0.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ High-Resolution Image Synthesis with Latent Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> and Fig. <a href="#A8.F23" title="Figure 23 ‣ Appendix H Additional Qualitative Results ‣ High-Resolution Image Synthesis with Latent Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>.
For our those models trained on comparably small datasets, we additionally show nearest neighbors in VGG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">simonyan2015VGG</a></cite> feature space for samples from our models in Fig. <a href="#A8.F32" title="Figure 32 ‣ Appendix H Additional Qualitative Results ‣ High-Resolution Image Synthesis with Latent Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">32</span></a> - <a href="#A8.F34" title="Figure 34 ‣ Appendix H Additional Qualitative Results ‣ High-Resolution Image Synthesis with Latent Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a>.</p>
</div>
